  lines: [
    { number: 190, text: '' },
    {
      number: 191,
      text: 'This prevents authors from treating all feedback as mandatory and wasting time on optional suggestions.'
    },
    { number: 192, text: '' },
    {
      number: 193,
      text: "**Lead with what matters.** Order findings by leverage: correctness and security first, then structural regressions and missed simplifications, then everything else. Don't bury a real issue under cosmetic nits — a few high-conviction comments beat a long list. If you have one structural problem and ten nits, the structural problem *is* the review."
    },
    { number: 194, text: '' },
    { number: 195, text: '### Step 5: Verify the Verification' },
    { number: 196, text: '' },
    { number: 197, text: "Check the author's verification story:" },
    { number: 198, text: '' },
    { number: 199, text: '```' },
    { number: 200, text: '- What tests were run?' },
    { number: 201, text: '- Did the build pass?' },
    { number: 202, text: '- Was the change tested manually?' },
    { number: 203, text: '- Are there screenshots for UI changes?' },
    { number: 204, text: '- Is there a before/after comparison?' },
    { number: 205, text: '```' },
    { number: 206, text: '' },
    { number: 207, text: '## Multi-Model Review Pattern' },
    { number: 208, text: '' },
    {
      number: 209,
      text: 'Use different models for different review perspectives:'
    },
    { number: 210, text: '' },
    { number: 211, text: '```' },
    { number: 212, text: 'Model A writes the code' },
    { number: 213, text: '    │' },
    { number: 214, text: '    ▼' },
    {
      number: 215,
      text: 'Model B reviews for correctness and architecture'
    },
    { number: 216, text: '    │' },
    { number: 217, text: '    ▼' },
    { number: 218, text: 'Model A addresses the feedback' },
    { number: 219, text: '    │' },
    { number: 220, text: '    ▼' },
    { number: 221, text: 'Human makes the final call' },
    { number: 222, text: '```' },
    { number: 223, text: '' },
    {
      number: 224,
      text: 'This catches issues that a single model might miss — different models have different blind spots.'
    },
    { number: 225, text: '' },
    { number: 226, text: '**Example prompt for a review agent:**' },
    { number: 227, text: '```' },
    {
      number: 228,
      text: 'Review this code change for correctness, security, and adherence to'
    },
    {
      number: 229,
      text: 'our project conventions. The spec says [X]. The change should [Y].'
    },
    {
      number: 230,
      text: 'Flag any issues as Critical, Required, Optional, or Nit.'
    },
    { number: 231, text: '```' },
    { number: 232, text: '' },
    { number: 233, text: '## Dead Code Hygiene' },
    { number: 234, text: '' },
    {
      number: 235,
      text: 'After any refactoring or implementation change, check for orphaned code:'
    },
    { number: 236, text: '' },
    {
      number: 237,
      text: '1. Identify code that is now unreachable or unused'
    },
    { number: 238, text: '2. List it explicitly' },
    {
      number: 239,
      text: '3. **Ask before deleting:** "Should I remove these now-unused elements: [list]?"'
    },
    { number: 240, text: '' },
    {
      number: 241,
      text: "Don't leave dead code lying around — it confuses future readers and agents. But don't silently delete things you're not sure about. When in doubt, ask."
    },
    { number: 242, text: '' },
    { number: 243, text: '```' },
    { number: 244, text: 'DEAD CODE IDENTIFIED:' },
    {
      number: 245,
      text: '- formatLegacyDate() in src/utils/date.ts — replaced by formatDate()'
    },
    {
      number: 246,
      text: '- OldTaskCard component in src/components/ — replaced by TaskCard'
    },
    {
      number: 247,
      text: '- LEGACY_API_URL constant in src/config.ts — no remaining references'
    },
    { number: 248, text: '→ Safe to remove these?' },
    { number: 249, text: '```' },
    { number: 250, text: '' },
    { number: 251, text: '## Review Speed' },
    { number: 252, text: '' },
    {
      number: 253,
      text: 'Slow reviews block entire teams. The cost of context-switching to review is less than the waiting cost imposed on others.'
    },
    { number: 254, text: '' },
    {
      number: 255,
      text: '- **Respond within one business day** — this is the maximum, not the target'
    },
    {
      number: 256,
      text: '- **Ideal cadence:** Respond shortly after a review request arrives, unless deep in focused coding. A typical change should complete multiple review rounds in a single day'
    },
    {
      number: 257,
      text: '- **Prioritize fast individual responses** over quick final approval. Quick feedback reduces frustration even if multiple rounds are needed'
    },
    {
      number: 258,
      text: '- **Large changes:** Ask the author to split them rather than reviewing one massive changeset'
    },
    { number: 259, text: '' },
    { number: 260, text: '## Handling Disagreements' },
    { number: 261, text: '' },
    {
      number: 262,
      text: 'When resolving review disputes, apply this hierarchy:'
    },
    { number: 263, text: '' },
    {
      number: 264,
      text: '1. **Technical facts and data** override opinions and preferences'
    },
    {
      number: 265,
      text: '2. **Style guides** are the absolute authority on style matters'
    },
    {
      number: 266,
      text: '3. **Software design** must be evaluated on engineering principles, not personal preference'
    },
    {
      number: 267,
      text: "4. **Codebase consistency** is acceptable if it doesn't degrade overall health"
    },
    { number: 268, text: '' },
    {
      number: 269,
      text: `**Don't accept "I'll clean it up later."** Experience shows deferred cleanup rarely happens. Require cleanup before submission unless it's a genuine emergency. If surrounding issues can't be addressed in this change, require filing a bug with self-assignment.`
    },
    { number: 270, text: '' },
    { number: 271, text: '## Honesty in Review' },
    { number: 272, text: '' },
    {
      number: 273,
      text: 'When reviewing code — whether written by you, another agent, or a human:'
    },
    { number: 274, text: '' },
    {
      number: 275,
      text: `- **Don't rubber-stamp.** "LGTM" without evidence of review helps no one.`
    },
    {
      number: 276,
      text: `- **Don't soften real issues.** "This might be a minor concern" when it's a bug that will hit production is dishonest.`
    },
    {
      number: 277,
      text: '- **Quantify problems when possible.** "This N+1 query will add ~50ms per item in the list" is better than "this could be slow."'
    },
    {
      number: 278,
      text: '- **Push back on approaches with clear problems.** Sycophancy is a failure mode in reviews. If the implementation has issues, say so directly and propose alternatives.'
    },
    {
      number: 279,
      text: '- **Accept override gracefully.** If the author has full context and disagrees, defer to their judgment. Comment on code, not people — reframe personal critiques to focus on the code itself.'
    },
    { number: 280, text: '' },
    { number: 281, text: '## Dependency Discipline' },
    { number: 282, text: '' },
    { number: 283, text: 'Part of code review is dependency review:' },
    { number: 284, text: '' },
    { number: 285, text: '**Before adding any dependency:**' },
    {
      number: 286,
      text: '1. Does the existing stack solve this? (Often it does.)'
    },
    {
      number: 287,
      text: '2. How large is the dependency? (Check bundle impact.)'
    },
    {
      number: 288,
      text: '3. Is it actively maintained? (Check last commit, open issues.)'
    },
    {
      number: 289,
      text: '4. Does it have known vulnerabilities? (`npm audit`)'
    },
    ... 109 more items
  ],
  totalLines: 398
}
